<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1063</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Abbaye aux Hommes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbey of Saint-Étienne, known as the Abbaye aux Hommes, is a former Benedictine abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> around 1063, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanfranc">Lanfranc</a> as its first abbot. Its church, consecrated in 1077, is a major example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-architecture">Norman Romanesque</a> architecture. William was buried there in 1087, and the monastic buildings now house the town hall of Caen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-etienne-caen.jpg" alt="Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chevet_abbHommes.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The abbey, dedicated to Saint Stephen, was founded to the west of Caen Castle at the same time as the Abbey of Sainte-Trinité, the Abbaye aux Dames, to its east. Together they were meant to strengthen the new ducal capital, and they may have formed part of the reconciliation between William, Duke of Normandy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-ix">Pope Leo IX</a>, who had forbidden William's marriage to his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-flanders">Matilda of Flanders</a>. Lanfranc of Pavia, Prior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bec-abbey">Bec Abbey</a>, secured the pope's forgiveness and was rewarded with the abbacy, which he held until he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> in 1070.</p><p>The foundation was also part of a wider programme of monastic reform in Normandy. Building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> stone began in 1066, and for decades the abbey and its sister house competed with each other in a way that profited both.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The west façade was finished at the end of the 11th century. In about 1120 both abbey churches received ribbed vaults, used here for the first time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the two buildings are considered forerunners of the Gothic. In 1166 the original Romanesque apse was replaced by an early Gothic chevet with rose windows and flying buttresses, and nine towers and spires were added in the 13th century. Inside, the vaulting runs from sexpartite vaults with circular ribs in the nave to quadripartite vaults with pointed ribs in the sanctuary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>William's funeral in 1087 was interrupted by a citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> who claimed his family had been unlawfully stripped of the land under the church; the claim was found to be true and he was compensated. The tomb was opened on papal orders in 1522 and again in 1562, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a>, when the black marble tombstone was destroyed and the bones were scattered except for one thigh bone. That bone was reburied in 1642, and the present white marble ledger stone dates from the early 19th century.</p><p>Iconoclasts looted the abbey during the Wars of Religion, and its central lantern tower collapsed and was never rebuilt. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> ended monastic life, and the church became a parish church in 1790. From 1804 to 1961 the buildings housed the Lycée Malherbe, and during the Normandy Landings people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> sheltered in the church, whose roof bore a red cross made with blood on a sheet to mark it as a hospital.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abbey of Saint-Étienne, Caen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Norman Romanesque architecture, seen at Jumièges, at <b>Saint-Étienne</b> in Caen and in the cathedrals of England, shaped building across the Anglo-Norman world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
